@* Comparing the two programs.
\label{compare}There are now two implementations of one program, and a course can use them
in three ways, from the most informal to the most precise.

\emph{Reading.} Because every file of the port has a counterpart with the same name and the
same functions, the two can be read side by side, and this document helps: in the index
of module names, a module ``used in'' two sections belongs to both versions, and a module
marked ``(macOS)'' or ``(Metal)'' is the counterpart of the module of the first part with
the same name. The introductions of the macOS parts list the correspondences between the
interfaces, notably the dictionary of Direct3D and Metal terms in the part on the
renderer.

\emph{Running.} Both programs start with the same material, shape, camera, light and
settings, and their panels are laid out the same way, so the first image of each is the
same picture. Pinning the probe on the same texel makes the inspectors print the same
numbers, and the arrows land on the same point. The images differ in small ways that are
worth discussing with students: the fonts; the thickness of the lines, which are one
\emph{pixel} wide and therefore look thinner on a Retina display; and the last bit of some
pixels, because two GPUs need not round the filtering of textures, the multisampling and
the arithmetic of the shaders identically. Nothing in the specifications of Direct3D or
Metal requires bit-identical images.

\emph{Measuring.} The CPU half of the program---shapes, sampling and the inspector---\emph{can}
be compared exactly, and the folder \texttt{mac/compare} contains a program that does it.
The difficulty is that the two versions never run on the same computer. But the CPU half
of the Windows version uses only standard C++, DirectXMath and a few declarations of
Direct3D; DirectXMath is a header-only library that Microsoft publishes for other
platforms too; and the Direct3D declarations can be replaced by stand-ins. So the very
files \texttt{src/Mesh.cpp}, \texttt{src/Material.cpp} and \texttt{src/Inspector.cpp},
unmodified, can be compiled on a Mac, next to their macOS counterparts.

@ The program |probe| is compiled twice from one source file: once against the Windows
sources and once against the port. Each copy prints the same report, and a small script
compares the two reports number by number.

\begin{verbatim}
cmake -S mac/compare -B mac/compare/build
cmake --build mac/compare/build --target compare
\end{verbatim}

At the time of writing, the result is this:

\medskip
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\textbf{quantity} & \textbf{largest difference} \\
mesh sizes and indices & 0 (identical) \\
vertex positions, normals, tangents & $2\cdot10^{-7}$ \\
vertex texture coordinates & 0 \\
colour, bump, displacement, roughness at the probe & 0 \\
slope of the height field & 0 \\
tangent-space normal $\vect n_{ts}$ & $2\cdot10^{-7}$ \\
world vectors $\vect N$, $\vect N'$, $\vect T$, $\vect B$ & $2\cdot10^{-7}$ \\
world position of the probe & $1\cdot10^{-6}$ \\
\end{tabular}
\medskip

The values read from the maps agree exactly, as they must, since they are computed by the
same module with the same operations. The vectors differ in the last bit or two of a
|float|, $2^{-23}\approx1.2\cdot10^{-7}$: DirectXMath and \texttt{simd} do the same
arithmetic, but a normalization, for example, may multiply by a reciprocal square root
in one library and divide by a square root in the other.

