@ \emph{The probe.} It prints three kinds of lines: the sizes of the four meshes, every
997th vertex of each, and the full report of the inspector on a grid of texels for every
material and shape---the corners of an $8\times8$ grid, borders included, where wrapping
matters, and a second grid that falls between texel centres, where the interpolation
matters. Each line begins with a key (the kind of line, the material, the shape, the
texel) so that the reports can be matched line by line.

The only differences between the two compilations are kept in preprocessor conditionals:
the Windows version takes its world matrix from DirectXMath and loads its materials
through the stand-in device; the macOS version creates a Metal device and queue. The
macOS probe must also compile the implementation of metal-cpp, which in the program is
done by \texttt{Renderer.cpp}.

<<file:mac/compare/probe.cpp>>=
